.TH SREC2FLEX 1 2026-10-26 plm09 "plm09 Manual"
.SH NAME
srec2flex \- convert S-records to a FLEX binary (.CMD) file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B srec2flex
.I in.s19
.I out.cmd
.br
.B srec2flex
.BR \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B srec2flex
turns Motorola S-records, as
.B "lwasm \-f srec"
writes them, into a FLEX binary file, which FLEX 9 loads and runs as a
command.
.BR lwasm (1)
has no FLEX output format of its own.
.PP
Each run of consecutive loaded bytes becomes $02 records (load address,
count and data) of at most 252 bytes, in address order, as FLEX's SAVE
command writes them.
The S9 record's address becomes the $16 transfer address record that
makes the file a command; an S9 record is therefore required (lwasm
writes one for
.BI "END " label ).
S0 and S5 records are ignored, and every record's checksum is verified.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was converted.
.TP
.B 1
A malformed record, a bad checksum, an unsupported record type (S2, S3,
S7, S8) or no S9 record.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, or a file that couldn't be read or written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build a FLEX command from a PL/M program:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plmc \-o hello.asm hello.plm
lwasm \-9 \-f srec \-I runtime \-I runtime/flex \-o hello.s19 hello.asm
srec2flex hello.s19 HELLO.CMD
.EE
.RE
.PP
Then copy HELLO.CMD to a FLEX disk, or run it with
.BR "sim6809 \-\-flex HELLO.CMD" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR plmc (1),
.BR sim6809 (1)
.PP
The FLEX section of the plmc manual,
.I docs/plmc.pdf
in the plm09 source tree.
